---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import TopologyFigure from '../../components/TopologyFigure.astro';
import ArchitectureExplorer from '../../components/ArchitectureExplorer.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'The challenge' },
  { id: 'architecture', label: 'Architecture explorer' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions & trade-offs' },
  { id: 'investigation', label: 'Diagnostic notebook' },
  { id: 'lessons', label: 'What I take forward' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Zero-Trust Cloud Architecture — Pratik Deshmukh"
  description="Inside my personal OCI infrastructure: Linux, containerized workloads, public ingress, private administration and a CPU troubleshooting investigation."
  canonicalPath="/work/zero-trust-cloud/"
  sections={sections}
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow case-project-label">01 / PERSONAL INFRASTRUCTURE PROJECT</span>
    <h1 id="case-title">
      Public by intention.
      <br />
      <span class="accent">Private by design.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Separating public traffic, administrative access and compute responsibilities in my personal
      OCI environment.
    </p>
    <div class="case-meta">
      <div>
        <small class="muted">ENVIRONMENT</small>
        <b>Oracle Cloud Infrastructure</b>
      </div>
      <div>
        <small class="muted">FOCUS</small>
        <b>Linux, networking & operations</b>
      </div>
      <div>
        <small class="muted">APPROACH</small>
        <b>Separate roles. Clear boundaries.</b>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <TopologyFigure slot="figure" />

  <section id="challenge" aria-labelledby="challenge-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">01 / THE CHALLENGE</span>
    <h2 id="challenge-title">
      Different workloads.
      <br />
      Different boundaries.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I built this environment to run and administer containerized services across ARM64 and x86
      compute. The interesting work was deciding what belonged together, what needed to remain
      private, and how to see what was happening when resources became constrained.
    </p>
    <p>
      The core node has more capacity for applications and observability. Fractional micro instances
      take on smaller edge responsibilities. Public web ingress and administrative access follow
      separate paths, rather than treating every service as something to expose.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is hands-on personal infrastructure work: Linux administration, container management,
      networking and diagnostics in the same environment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">02 / EXPLORE THE ARCHITECTURE</span>
    <h2 id="architecture-title">
      Three nodes.
      <br />
      Distinct responsibilities.
    </h2>
    <p>
      Monster runs the core applications and observability stack. Phoenix handles public ingress.
      Wraith takes on asynchronous edge workloads. Select a node to inspect its compute, services
      and role in the wider system.
    </p>
    <ArchitectureExplorer />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">03 / DECISIONS & TRADE-OFFS</span>
    <h2 id="decisions-title">
      The boundary matters
      <br />
      more than the diagram.
    </h2>
    <h3>A private mesh for administration</h3>
    <p>
      I used Tailscale for inter-server connectivity and private access to administrative tools such
      as Grafana and Portainer. Keeping those tools on private mesh addresses reduces the need to
      expose their interfaces directly to public traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
      Private DNS makes the tools easier to reach by name; it is not an authorization mechanism.
      Reachability, service authentication and access policy are separate parts of the boundary.
    </p>
    <h3>A dedicated public ingress role</h3>
    <p>
      Cloudflared on Phoenix provides the public tunnel ingress role, with Nginx on the edge node.
      Nginx Proxy Manager sits on Monster. Keeping ingress distinct from core applications makes
      those responsibilities easier to explain and troubleshoot.
    </p>
    <p>
      The architecture view focuses on these roles and access relationships rather than exposing
      individual application mappings or administrative endpoints.
    </p>
    <h3>Visibility has a resource cost</h3>
    <p>
      Prometheus and Grafana provide metrics collection and visualization; Node Exporter and Dozzle
      agents support host metrics and log visibility on the edge nodes. Portainer supports container
      administration, while Restic is part of the backup tooling.
    </p>
    <p>
      These tools help inspect the environment, but collection and polling consume resources too. On
      a fractional instance, operational tooling belongs in the performance investigation alongside
      the application workload.
    </p>
    <h3>Host networking changes the trade-off</h3>
    <p>
      Moving a container to host networking removes the separate container network namespace. That
      changes both the networking path and the isolation boundary, so a performance change needs to
      be assessed alongside bind addresses, port conflicts and access controls.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="investigation" aria-labelledby="investigation-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">04 / DIAGNOSTIC NOTEBOOK</span>
    <h2 id="investigation-title">
      Investigating CPU steal.
      <br />
      Keeping the conclusion precise.
    </h2>
    <div class="capability">
      <span>Observation</span>
      <p>
        I investigated CPU steal-time degradation reported above 40% on fractional micro instances,
        alongside the overhead of continuous telemetry.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="capability">
      <span>Intervention</span>
      <p>
        I moved continuous-polling telemetry containers from Docker bridge networking to host
        networking to investigate the networking overhead.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="capability">
      <span>Recorded result</span>
      <p>
        My project notes record a 95% reduction in kernel-level context switches. The original
        sampling windows and comparable workload traces are not retained here, so this figure is a
        reported observation rather than a reproducible benchmark.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="capability">
      <span>Conclusion</span>
      <p>
        Context switches and CPU steal time describe different behavior. Fewer context switches
        alone do not demonstrate that hypervisor scheduling delays were reduced or permanently
        resolved.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The useful lesson is methodological: keep the symptom, intervention and measured signal
      separate. A repeatable comparison needs matched workloads, sampling intervals and CPU
      accounting before drawing a stronger causal conclusion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lessons" aria-labelledby="lessons-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">05 / WHAT I TAKE FORWARD</span>
    <h2 id="lessons-title">
      Know the system.
      <br />
      Question the measurement.
    </h2>
    <p>
      This work brought together workload placement, private access, container administration and
      operational visibility. It gave me practical reasons to think about where a service runs, how
      it is reached and what its supporting tools cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next useful step is stronger operational evidence: repeatable performance traces and
      documented recovery exercises. Clear boundaries make a system easier to reason about; good
      measurements make the explanation more dependable.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/work/microsoft-365-identity/">
      Next: Microsoft 365 administration sandbox ↗
    </a>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-project-label {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
</style>
